Chronic Pelvic Pain Syndrome (CPPS)
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Chronic perineal/pelvic pain >3 months, Pain with ejaculation, Tender pelvic floor musculature, Negative prostatic fluid cultures

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-008
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute exacerbation of perineal pain and severe dysuria causing profound distress and fear of acute urinary retention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute, intense perineal and suprapubic pain with severe irritative voiding symptoms, leading to high anxiety regarding potential urinary retention or acute severe infection. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out acute bacterial prostatitis, acute epididymo-orchitis, and acute urinary retention via physical examination prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It feels like I'm sitting on a golf ball made of glass, and it burns terribly every time I try to pee or ejaculate."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 4-day severe exacerbation of persistent, episodic pain in the perineum and suprapubic region, present for the last 5 months. The patient describes exquisite discomfort and sharp pain immediately following ejaculation. Current symptoms include severe irritative voiding symptoms (increased frequency, severe urgency, and dysuria). The symptoms sharply escalated following a period of high psychological stress at work. The patient completely denies high fevers, gross hematuria, or complete inability to pass urine.
Functional Status: Independent but severely distressed and pacing due to perineal pressure.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety and catastrophic thinking regarding potential malignancy or permanent sexual dysfunction.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/88 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, refusing to sit down, pacing the room.
Physical Exam: Abdomen soft, non-tender, bladder not palpably distended. Genitourinary exam reveals normal testes and epididymides bilaterally with no focal tenderness or swelling. Digital rectal examination reveals a completely normal-feeling prostate gland without significant boggy swelling, focal nodules, or acute extreme tenderness. However, the surrounding pelvic floor musculature is excessively tender and hypertonic upon digital palpation. Urinalysis via dipstick is completely normal, lacking leukocytes or nitrites. Documented history reveals entirely negative expressed prostatic secretion (EPS) cultures.
Environmental Observations: Home office environment indicates long hours of continuous sitting.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Pelvic Pain Syndrome (CPPS) - Status: Acute hypertonic flare
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Prostatitis - Ruled out due to absence of fever, normal urinalysis, and absence of an exquisitely tender, boggy prostate on DRE.

Acute Urinary Retention - Ruled out as the patient is actively passing urine and the bladder is not palpably distended.

Epididymo-orchitis - Ruled out due to completely normal, non-tender scrotal examination.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to a severe, acute exacerbation of perineal pain and irritative voiding symptoms that mimicked an acute genitourinary infection or impending urinary retention. The severity of the pain and the patient's profound anxiety necessitated an immediate physical examination, including a digital rectal examination and urinalysis, to clinically exclude acute bacterial prostatitis and retention. By confirming the functional, hypertonic nature of the pain and the absence of acute infection, the physician safely prevented a highly distressed and unnecessary presentation to the emergency department, satisfying the criteria for an urgent MBS Item 597 encounter.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of NSAIDs (Ibuprofen 400mg TDS) and an alpha-blocker (Tamsulosin 400mcg daily) to relax the prostatic smooth muscle and bladder neck.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a strict routine of taking a 5-minute walking or stretching break for every 45 minutes of sitting. Relieving the mechanical pressure on the hypertonic pelvic floor will immediately reduce the 'golf ball' sensation, positively reinforcing the habit of breaking up prolonged sitting.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized pelvic floor physiotherapist.
DME/Safety (Optional): Donut or cut-out cushion for desk chair.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 2 weeks to assess response to alpha-blocker therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing high fevers, rigors, gross blood in the urine, or complete absolute inability to pass urine for more than 6 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes previously negative prostatic fluid cultures remain representative; if symptoms change to include fever, re-testing is mandatory.
Clinical Course: CPPS is a chronic tension disorder of the pelvic floor muscles, not an active infection. It severely impacts quality of life but is benign and responds well to targeted physiotherapy and muscle relaxation strategies.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you develop a high fever, start passing visible blood in your urine, or if you become completely unable to empty your bladder.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain is caused by the muscles in your pelvis cramping and spasming tightly around your nerves and bladder, much like a tension headache in the pelvis. Antibiotics will not cure muscle spasm.
Actionable Steps: Use a hot bath or a heating pad on your perineum for 15 minutes every evening. The targeted heat will actively relax the spastic musculature, immediately lowering your pain score and reinforcing the use of thermal relaxation over catastrophizing.

